   "body": "**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, a216cab56f1f51d04, round 2)**\n\n**Adversarial review (Opus 5.5, round 2): APPROVE** — head fb3e27f\n\n1. **Low**, PR description. It no longer matches head fb3e27f:\n   - It still names the old test, `ReaderWhitelistContrastWebTests`.\n   - It says the classic dark date \"rises to 7.07\". That is wrong now: the dark date stays in `--text`, as #1210 set it.\n   - It says two light cases fail on `dev-swift`. Three fail: the modern light quote header is at 4.45.\n   - The neighbouring cases from round 1 are not listed yet: the blocked quote in a moderation message (4.23:1) and the date of a continuation of an allowlisted classic-light message (3.28:1).\n   - Fix: rewrite the description and post the two follow-ups on #893 (or open an issue) before merge.\n2. **Detail**, `reader.css:319-322`. The figures in the comment are right, but \"5.67 and 5.15\" are the classic figures. The modern quote header goes from 4.45 to 5.67, which the comment does not say. Optional.\n\nRound 1 rechecked:\n- (1) Lint is fixed.\n- (2) Fixed: the classic date rule is inside `@media not (prefers-color-scheme: dark)`, so #1210's `.style-classic .message .date` (`--text`) still wins in dark. The new flag `seen[2]` checks this, and the test fails if the media wrapper is removed.\n- (3) The comment's figures match my own calculation.\n- (4) The neighbouring cases are still owed (finding 1).\n\n**Checked**\n- `swiftlint lint --strict` on `App/SwiftHFR/Reader/` and `App/SwiftHFRTests/Reader/` (worktree at fb3e27f): exit 0, no line over 140.\n- Contrast ratios recomputed with Python. Light, `--secondary` vs the new colour: classic date 4.45 → 5.67; classic quote header 4.05 → 5.15; modern quote header 4.45 → 5.67.\n- Dark (page `#2e2f33`): modern 6.37, classic 6.65.\n- Other surfaces with the new colour, all at 5.51 or more: the « Doux » light style, a blocked quote inside an allowed quote, a black dark page.\n- Specificity: the quote rule is (0,4,4) against `b.s1` at (0,1,1); the light date rule is (0,5,0).\n- The test fails without the change: three light assertions fall under 4.5. `QuoteMarkup` leaves the hand-written `quote-blocked` class in place, and a missing element means a recorded failure, not a silent pass.\n- Swift reviewed line by line (Linux, not built): it follows `ReaderSeparationWebTests`, and the memberwise init takes `style` then `visibility` in declaration order.\n\n---\n_Generated by [Claude Code](https://claude.ai/code)_",
